# Start and Address Agents

This Livebook starts Agents in partitions and uses the public lookup and list
APIs.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, path: Path.expand("..", __DIR__)}
])
```

## Define A Small Agent

```elixir
defmodule AddressGuide.Worker do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "address_guide_worker"

  agent do
    schema Zoi.object(%{label: Zoi.string() |> Zoi.default("worker")})
  end
end
```

## Start Two Partitions

```elixir
{:ok, _jido} = Jido.start()
jido = Jido.default_instance()

{:ok, east} =
  Jido.start_agent(jido, AddressGuide.Worker,
    id: "worker-1",
    partition: :east
  )

{:ok, west} =
  Jido.start_agent(jido, AddressGuide.Worker,
    id: "worker-1",
    partition: :west
  )

{east, west}
```

An Agent ID is unique inside one Jido instance and partition. A partition is a
logical namespace. It is not an authorization boundary.

## Look Up And List

```elixir
^east = Jido.whereis_agent(jido, "worker-1", partition: :east)
^west = Jido.whereis_agent(jido, "worker-1", partition: :west)

%{
  east: Jido.list_agents(jido, partition: :east),
  west: Jido.list_agents(jido, partition: :west),
  counts: %{
    east: Jido.agent_count(jido, partition: :east),
    west: Jido.agent_count(jido, partition: :west)
  }
}
```

Use the same instance and partition for start, lookup, relationships, and
persistence keys.

## Stop The Agents

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(jido, east)
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(jido, west)
:ok = Jido.stop()
```

Local Registry uniqueness does not establish cluster-exclusive ownership. See
[Extension Boundaries](extension-boundaries.md) before you activate the same
business identity on more than one node.
